1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,320
 In the last video on our series of FRPs we're going to talk briefly about embedded fiber

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:08,880
 reinforced plastics. These plastics are made by variety of techniques, some of them very similar

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:13,840
 or completely identical to the manufacturing processes we talked about for plastics all of those

4
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:18,400
 weeks ago and some of them were unique to fiber reinforced variants. On one end of this spectrum

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:23,440
 some finished fiber reinforced plastic parts are made using injection moulding. Here small,

6
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:28,480
 chopped and reasonably randomly oriented fibers are distributed within a thermoplastic matrix and

7
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:32,400
 injected into a mould to form a part. The considerations for doing so in the machinery

8
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:36,240
 required are pretty much the same as regular injection moulding. Though there is a practical

9
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:40,240
 limitation on how much fiber contact can be present in your plastic for flowability of the

10
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:45,440
 plastic into the mould usually about 60%. Though it's not only a function of volume as longer

11
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,440
 fibers tend to cause more problems than shorter ones. Any point in consideration when injection

12
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:54,480
 moulding fiber reinforced plastics is accidental or intentional alignment of the fibers with the

13
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:58,720
 direction of flow. Particularly for smaller fibers that you get kind of carried away with the

14
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:03,280
 current you sometimes see alignment of these fibers in the direction that plastic flows within the

15
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,880
 mould. And the result of this can be a little bit like the texture that you see in some forged

16
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:11,360
 metal components resulting in an effective stiffening and strengthening of the part in the

17
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:15,680
 direction of the fibers in the direction of flow. This obviously results in anisotropy of the

18
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:20,000
 properties which can sometimes be beneficial and sometimes be detrimental. On the other end of the

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,320
 spectrum there exist manufacturing processes that are very much specific to fiber reinforced

20
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:29,280
 plastics. For example many fiber reinforced plastic standard sections like bars and rods are made

21
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:34,720
 through a process known as protrusion. Here in a process very similar to extrusion plastic is

22
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:39,120
 forced through a die though in this case fibers are also pulled through the die at the same time.

23
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:43,040
 Parts that are made by protrusion tend to be quite anisotropic in the direction of the

24
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:47,440
 major axis of the part because all the fibers which are themselves quite long end up aligned

25
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,880
 with this direction. These can be quite beneficial if you plan to use these plastics in an

26
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:55,200
 axial or bending loading scenario but can be quite detrimental otherwise and you need to be a

27
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,880
 little bit careful. Whether they're injection mould with small fibers or they're protruded with

28
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,600
 long fibers many fiber reinforced plastics can be machined the same as regular plastics.

29
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:07,520
 There is some additional care required here though because some of the fibers can be quite

30
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:12,240
 hard you may see an unexpected amount of toolware when machining these compared to same machining

31
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:16,160
 regular plastics that don't put up much of a fight at all. And depending on the particular

32
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:20,560
 type of FRP and the tooling that you're using in your feeds and speeds you may also find that

33
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,280
 in machining you pull out some fibers that ruin the surface finish and introduce the stress

34
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:29,280
 raises on your part. On top of that machining these parts and releasing all the small bits of

35
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:33,520
 fiber into the air can be quite dangerous for both the machine and the operators so appropriate

36
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:37,520
 PPE is required. I guess the general message is that it's a little bit more complicated and you

37
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:41,280
 need to be a little bit more careful but it can be done and there are some FRP's that are

38
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:44,960
 specifically designed for machining ability. And that's where I might leave it bringing this little

39
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:48,880
 mini-series to a close. I hope you enjoyed it. I know it's a little bit more in depth than we've

40
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:53,040
 got in the past in some manufacturing techniques but it should give you a pretty good appreciation

41
00:02:53,040 --> 00:03:04,800
 for some of the different approaches you have when manufacturing with fiber reinforced polymers.
